<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akkadian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Akkadians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Akkadian Empire was the first known empire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> around the twenty-fourth century BCE. It united the Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> and extended its influence over neighbouring regions, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>. It declined amid revolts, invasions and climatic stress such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/4-2-kiloyear-event">4.2-kiloyear event</a>, and was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire.jpg" alt="Akkadian Empire" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Zunkir · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empire_akkad.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Akkadian Empire takes its name from the city and region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, near the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. The city has not yet been identified on the ground, but it is known from textual sources, at least one of them older than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a>'s reign. The period is generally dated to 2334–2154 BCE by the middle chronology; the short-chronology dates of 2270–2083 BCE are now considered less likely. It followed the Early Dynastic Period and was succeeded by the Ur III Period, though both transitions are blurry.</p><p>Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a>, was a usurper, and he is not known to have had a father or predecessor on the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, until then a marginal city. Later texts make it hard to separate legend from reality. Some historians, drawing on a late legend, suggest he rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-zababa">Ur-Zababa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, but this is hypothetical. He then faced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugal-zage-si">Lugal-zage-si</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, the most powerful king in the south, and according to copies of his inscriptions defeated him around 2300 BCE. Lugal-zage-si was captured, made to wear a yoke and paraded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> installed loyal governors in several Sumerian cities and so created a kingdom covering all of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> as its capital. His influence is said to have stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and expeditions went as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magan">Magan</a>. His sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimush">Rimush</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manishtushu">Manishtushu</a> struggled to keep control, and both may have died violently. Sargon's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> called himself King of the Four Quarters and had himself called god of Akkad, with a cult in his honour, though whether he was considered a full deity is debated.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a>'s successors were weaker. The empire collapsed, probably through a combination of internal unrest, severe environmental and economic stress from a drought associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/4-2-kiloyear-event">4.2-kiloyear climate event</a>, and threats from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutians">Gutians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-language">Akkadian language</a> became the empire's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>, while Sumerian stayed important in religion and literature. The kings had their exploits written in long inscriptions on stelae and statues, and their royal art, with a more naturalistic sculpture centred on the king, influenced later Mesopotamian art. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna">Enheduanna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a>'s daughter, served as high priestess and is often recognised as the first known named author in history. The kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> stood in the tradition of the first empire. Later, Sargon became the example to follow and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> the example to avoid, and several rulers took the title King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Akkadian Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He used the collapse of the <b>Akkadian Empire</b> to free Susa, brought Anshan and the surrounding highlands under his rule, and campaigned deep into northern Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Akkadian Empire</b> Lugal-ushumgal and Puzur-Mama governed Lagash for Naram-Sin and Shar-Kali-Sharri; after Shar-Kali-Sharri's death Puzur-Mama declared the city independent, though the Akkadian ruler Dudu still reported taking booty there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Neo-Sumerian Empire, also known as the Third Dynasty of Ur (Ur III), arose around 2112 BCE, after the collapse of the <b>Akkadian Empire</b> and the political fragmentation that followed in Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Akkadian Empire</b> fell in the 22nd century BCE to internal strain and to the Gutians from the Zagros Mountains, and it is not known whether a Gutian king or a local governor then ruled Ur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Akkadian Empire</b> and later the Third Dynasty of Ur, Uruk was ruled by governors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>A cylinder seal of the time of the <b>Akkadian Empire</b>, now in the Louvre, belonged to a man named Shu-ilishu, who called himself “interpreter of the Meluhhan language”: Mesopotamians needed translators to deal with traders from the Indus.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
